CAT = "sqeTrusts"; SEG = "TRU"
Q = [
("A will says: I hope my son will look after my sister. Which certainty is likely missing?",
 "Intention", ["Subject matter", "Objects", "Compliance"],
 "Precatory words of hope or wish do not show an intention to impose a trust; the son probably takes absolutely."),
("A discretionary trust is for 'my employees and their relatives'. What test applies to the objects?",
 "Is or is not test", ["Complete list test", "Any one person test", "Majority of class test"],
 "Under McPhail v Baden it must be possible to say of any given person whether they are or are not in the class. Fixed trusts need a complete list."),
("A donor signed and delivered a share transfer form but died before the company registered it. What is the gift?",
 "Complete in equity", ["Void for lack of form", "Imperfect and failed", "A resulting trust"],
 "Under Re Rose a gift is complete in equity once the donor has done everything in their power to transfer the property."),
("A declaration of trust of land is made orally. What is needed for it to be enforceable?",
 "Signed written evidence", ["A deed witnessed twice", "Registration at once", "A court order"],
 "Section 53(1)(b) LPA 1925 requires a declaration of trust of land to be manifested and proved by writing signed by the person able to declare it."),
("A beneficiary wants to transfer her existing equitable interest to a friend. What form is needed?",
 "Signed writing", ["A deed", "Oral agreement", "Court approval"],
 "Section 53(1)(c) LPA 1925 requires a disposition of a subsisting equitable interest to be in writing signed by the person disposing of it, or it is void."),
("For a fully secret trust to be valid, when must the terms be communicated and accepted?",
 "In the testator's lifetime", ["Before probate", "Within a year of death", "At the reading of the will"],
 "A fully secret trust needs intention, communication to the legatee and acceptance (even silent) during the testator's lifetime."),
("A man pays for a house put in his friend's name alone, with no evidence of intention. What is presumed?",
 "Resulting trust for him", ["Outright gift to the friend", "Constructive trust for both", "Equal shares for both"],
 "Where one person provides the purchase money and title goes to another, equity presumes a resulting trust in favour of the payer, unless rebutted."),
("An unmarried couple buy a home in joint names with no declaration of trust. What is the starting point?",
 "Equal beneficial shares", ["Shares by contribution", "All to the higher earner", "Resulting trust shares"],
 "Stack v Dowden and Jones v Kernott: in a joint names domestic case equity follows the law, so the starting presumption is equal shares unless shown otherwise."),
("A trustee uses trust information to make a personal profit, acting honestly. What must he do?",
 "Account for the profit", ["Keep it, as he was honest", "Split it with the trust", "Pay tax and keep the rest"],
 "A fiduciary must not profit from his position without authorisation; even an honest trustee must account for the profit (Boardman v Phipps)."),
("A trustee committed a breach while acting honestly and reasonably. What may the court do?",
 "Excuse him wholly or partly", ["Nothing, as liability is strict", "Remove all beneficiaries", "Order a criminal trial"],
 "Section 61 Trustee Act 1925 lets the court relieve a trustee who acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused."),
("All beneficiaries are adults of full capacity and together absolutely entitled. What can they do?",
 "End it and take the fund", ["Only change the trustees", "Nothing until it ends", "Ask the court to vary it"],
 "Under Saunders v Vautier beneficiaries who are together absolutely entitled and of full age and capacity can call for the trust property and end the trust."),
("A trustee mixes trust money with his own in one account, then spends some. Whose money is treated as spent first?",
 "The trustee's own", ["The trust's money", "Each in proportion", "Whichever came in first"],
 "Under Re Hallett a trustee is presumed to spend his own money first, so the beneficiaries can claim the balance left in the account."),
("A trust is set up to maintain a garden forever, and it is not charitable. Is it valid?",
 "No, it has no beneficiaries", ["Yes, as a purpose trust", "Yes, if the garden is public", "Only for 21 years"],
 "Non-charitable purpose trusts generally fail under the beneficiary principle because no one can enforce them; the anomalous exceptions do not cover a garden."),
("For a trust created after 1 October 2014, how much of a beneficiary's capital can trustees advance under s.32?",
 "All of it", ["Up to one half", "Up to one third", "None before age 25"],
 "The Inheritance and Trustees' Powers Act 2014 removed the one half limit for trusts created or arising after 1 October 2014."),
("A third party dishonestly helps a trustee misapply trust money, without receiving any. What is his liability?",
 "Personal liability to account", ["None, as he got nothing", "Only a criminal penalty", "A trustee de son tort"],
 "Dishonest assistance in a breach of trust makes the helper personally liable to account for the loss, without needing to receive property (Royal Brunei Airlines v Tan)."),
("Before investing trust money, what must trustees normally obtain under the Trustee Act 2000?",
 "Proper advice", ["Court approval", "All beneficiaries' consent", "A valuation of the trust"],
 "Section 5 requires trustees to obtain and consider proper advice before investing, unless they reasonably conclude it is unnecessary or inappropriate."),
]
